{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RP-03 Jira Cloud/DC failure-taxonomy checkpoint (2026-08-17).\n\nArtifacts: `.joe-janitor/rp03-jira-failure-taxonomy.md`, `.joe-janitor/rp03-jira-dc-hidden-retry-probe.py`, and `.joe-janitor/rp03-rate-limit-scope-probe.py`. The detailed research summary was also recorded on `rubellite-secondary-cub`. No production or tracked file changed.\n\nThe complete taxonomy classifies Jira failures by evidence, replay safety, healing action, and failure domain rather than status alone. Processing order: classify; determine replay safety; retry, defer, or re-observe; emit one terminal operation outcome; then feed that outcome to a scoped finite-pass fuse. Attempts never count toward the fuse. Typed outcomes include applied/already-satisfied, retryable-deferred with `retry_not_before`, `commit_unknown`, permanent operation failure, dependency deferred, scope deferred, and safety aborted.\n\nKey design correction: a definitive Jira rejection such as authoritative 429 is replayable after the provider delay, but a timeout/reset/malformed response after a possible write is `commit_unknown`. Create recovers by deterministic rebar identity and existing write-ahead/index-lag grace; comments require a deterministic operation marker before replay; links re-read/dedupe; transitions re-read status/legal transitions; desired-state updates re-read drift; deletes direct-GET and treat 404 as satisfied.\n\nScopes: Cloud per-issue 429 is ticket-local; burst is HTTP method+endpoint family; global/tenant quota defers the provider. Unknown Cloud 429 starts at endpoint family. DC rate limiting is per principal and node and must not consume Cloud reason semantics. `Retry-After` is a lower bound; a long delay becomes deferred `retry_not_before`, never a 60-second cap followed by an early retry. 401/CAPTCHA/TLS/invalid canonical URL or failed authenticated project preflight safety-abort the provider pass. Isolated 403/404/validation/conflict/payload failures remain ticket-local unless independent evidence proves shared loss. Maintenance can defer writes while allowing only authoritative safe reads. Incomplete/stalled/duplicate/contradictory pagination aborts the snapshot and can never produce deletion evidence.\n\nAuto-heal: immutable-ID re-key; direct reads/Cloud reconcile IDs for index lag; refresh/replan on 409/412 or metadata/workflow drift; smaller pages or POST for 413/414 without truncation; observation after ambiguous writes. Permanent: TLS/certificate, CAPTCHA, invalid URL/context/redirect, unsupported endpoint, repeated validation/capability/permission failures. Reads use bounded delayed retry for transient connectivity/408/429/5xx. A data-specific 500 on one issue does not prove provider outage.\n\nDirect code verification and executable probe exposed hidden DC attempts. Rebar omits `max_retries`, `timeout`, and `get_server_info` when constructing locked jira 3.10.5, whose defaults are 3 internal retries, no timeout, and constructor serverInfo I/O. Measured: persistent create 429 = 4 POSTs; create 503 = 4 POSTs; paged read 429 through nested layers = 12 GETs; disconnected create = 12 lower-level attempts. Fake-client tests do not see these attempts. Recommendation: DC `max_retries=0`, explicit connect/read timeouts, explicit bounded startup validation, and one coordinator retry owner.\n\nThe rate-scope probe also proved that raw Cloud errors retain `Retry-After` and `RateLimit-Reason`, DC error translation currently drops both, and ACLI text parsing retains delay but not reason. All three current paths can retry earlier than a positive long server delay.\n\nEvidence includes official Atlassian Cloud rate-reason guidance, DC per-user/per-node rate limiting, Cloud/DC auth and CAPTCHA guidance, Cloud enhanced-search consistency controls, and pycontribs user reports #1463, #1945, #1805, #1486, #1504, #1196, #733, #966, #1296, and #740. Maintained prior art: GitLab delayed bounded Jira retries; Sentry per-external-issue isolation with typed auth/config halts; controller-runtime per-object delayed requeue; Crossplane write-ahead create uncertainty plus re-observation.\n\nProposed fuse foundation: authoritative shared signals open their documented scope immediately; inferred provider outage requires terminal exhausted outcomes across at least two tickets and two endpoint families; scope success resets the matching consecutive count; a separate hard pass cap prevents alternating failures from consuming unbounded calls; permanent ticket failures and per-issue limits do not feed it. Exact numeric retry/fuse thresholds remain the next calibration step after maintainer approval of this architecture."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787000144758445001,"uuid":"b4ee694a-5107-4fee-a99b-766a2312ed7f"}